{
  "note": "Re-judged 2026-10-31 by an independent auditor who authored none of 6b0a55d9b9. RFC 2545 Section 3 read: \"The value of the Length of Next Hop Network Address field on a MP_REACH_NLRI attribute shall be set to 16, when only a global address is present, or 32 if a link-local address is also included in the Next Hop field.\" 6b0a55d9b9 changed only the RFC2545-3-1 comment of test/encode/new-v6.ci; the RFC2545-3-2 negative tag and every expect line are byte-identical, and the hex still pins the octet as 0x10 before one 16-octet global. That carrier proves nothing for 3-2 on its own: the journal row of 414927494d (reference-checked-claim-unchecked, owned by spec-bgp-one-off-defects) records that no break can discriminate it, because MPReachNLRI.WriteTo writes the octet from nextHopLen over the same NextHops list writeNextHops serialises, and a break of the octet alone yields a malformed attribute that writeUpdateGated refuses, so the run goes red by absence, not on the octet assertion. Its negative is also nominal: the input conforms and nothing violating is fed. So this verdict does not rest on new-v6.ci; the tag stays unproven in ./le rfc discriminate until the owed internal/core/bgp/attribute unit test of the octet WriteTo writes is tagged and recorded. What the verdict rests on is unchanged: positives whose oracle builds the octet independently (mpReachIPv6Attr computes byte(len(nh)) from the expected addresses, not from the encoder) pin 0x10 and 0x20 on the wire on the announce, default-originate, static-group, API, queued, commit and forward rails, plus conf-llnh-update.ci, adj-rib-in-replay-rfc2545-next-hop.ci and the FRR interop check checkRFC2545NextHops; negatives refuse every width outside 16 and 32 for IPv6 unicast (four-octet IPv4, 24/48-octet VPN shapes) and a 32-octet field whose second address is not link-local, on recorded carriers. Remaining limit, stated plainly: no recorded break anywhere isolates the octet write itself (buf[off+3] = byte(nhLen)); the stack cannot emit a mismatched octet because the octet and the field share one source and the writer gate refuses a malformed attribute, and the exact-byte positives would fail if one reached the wire. Verdict unchanged: enforced. Earlier judgements are in this file's git history.",
